"45% of remote MCP servers changed their tool definitions in four weeks. The log is now a dataset. | by Aleksandr Artamokhov | Oct, 2026 | Medium (https://medium.com/%40alexar76/45-of-remote-mcp-servers-changed-their-tool-definitions-in-four-weeks-the-log-is-now-a-dataset-71563ea5e5f2)\nciteturn12search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: last week; A server operator who wants to be left out of future snapshots can open an issue on alexar76/histor.\n\n# 45% of remote MCP servers changed their tool definitions in four weeks. The log is now a dataset.\n\nAleksandr Artamokhov\n\n4 min read\n\n·\n\nOct 20, 2026\n\n--\n\nListen\n\nShare\n\nPress enter or click to view image in full size\n\nA client that connects an MCP server reads its tool definitions: names, descriptions and input schemas. The model then reads them as instructions. Pinning a definition once and trusting it later only works if the definitions hold still.\n\nHISTOR, our public log of MCP servers, reads the tool list of every remote server in the official MCP registry once a day and records every change. As of today the whole record is downloadable as files. Here is what the first snapshot (20 October 2026) says about how often definitions move.\n\n## Half of them move\n\nTake the 10,929 remote servers that had a recorded tool list by 24 September and are still listed. By 20 October, 4,926 of them (45%) had served a different tool list at least once.\n\n  * 2,036 changed the description of a tool they already had.\n\nThe same files are on Hugging Face and archived on Zenodo, so a paper can cite them: doi:10.5281/zenodo.23512760 for this snapshot.\n\n  * `servers.jsonl.gz`: 83,957 rows (remote endpoints and packages, delisted ones included), with status, tool count and the digest of the current tool list\n  * `toolsets.jsonl.gz`: 57,846 tool lists exactly as served, current ones and every one a change replaced\n  * `changes.jsonl.gz`: 23,821 changes with tools added, removed and modified, field by field\n  * `scans.jsonl.gz` and `pattern-set.json`: WARDEN's pattern matches and the rules\n  * `manifest.json`: the SHA-256 of every file, signed by the log's key, with the signed tree head the snapshot was taken under\n\nFields that vary in shape are JSON text, so every file loads as a flat table. The 45% above is one DuckDB query:\n    \n    WITH base AS (\n      SELECT id FROM 'servers.jsonl.gz'\n      WHERE transport <> 'stdio' AND NOT delisted\n        AND toolSetDigest IS NOT NULL AND firstPinned <= '2026-09-24T23:59:59Z'\n    )\n    SELECT count(*) AS servers,\n           count(*) FILTER (WHERE id IN (\n             SELECT serverId FROM 'changes.jsonl.gz'\n             WHERE json_extract_string(summaryJson, '$.detail') = 'full')) AS changed\n    FROM base;\n    -- 10929 | 4926\n\nTo check one server without downloading anything: modelmarket.dev/tools/histor-check.\n\nWe build HISTOR and WARDEN. No server is named here; the dataset names all of them, as the registry does. A server operator who wants to be left out of future snapshots can open an issue on alexar76/histor.\n\nQuestions or corrections? The discussion is on dev.to.\n\nOriginally published at https://modelmarket.dev on October 20, 2026.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPingBlip — hatch a pet from real weather (free browser game) - Release Announcements - itch.io (https://itch.io/t/7175888/pingblip-hatch-a-pet-from-real-weather-free-browser-game)\nciteturn12search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # PingBlip — hatch a pet from real weather (free browser game)\n\n# PingBlip — hatch a pet from real weather (free browser game)\n\nA topic by PingBlip created 1 day ago (20 October 2026 @ 07:57 UTC) Views: 11\n\nHi everyone! We are sharing PingBlip, our free browser creature collector, now playable on itch.io.\n\nReal weather helps decide what hatches. Feed your pet with live sensor readings and discover a collection of 80 odd little creatures, including Worm CEO and Chonk Capy. When you are away, your pet falls asleep; it never dies.\n\nPlay PingBlip on itch.io — no sign-up or download. It works on phones and computers.\n\nImage: PingBlip game screenshot\n\nWe just simplified the first-pet flow and would love feedback from people trying it on a phone: is it clear what to tap first, and how to feed your new pet? If something gets in your way, please tell us the step and your browser.\n\nFor transparency, development uses AI-assisted code, procedural 3D asset scripts and text.\n\nThanks for trying it!\nAlex / PingBlip--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nalexar76 (Aleksandr Artamokhov) (https://huggingface.co/alexar76/models)\nciteturn12search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions\n\nImage: Aleksandr Artamokhov's picture\n\n# Aleksandr Artamokhov\n\nalexar76\n\nFollow\n\nhttps://modelmarket.dev/\n\n  * build_ai_infra\n  * alexar76\n\n### AI & ML interests\n\nBuilding the infrastructure for the AI agent economy — a self-hosted AI factory, a capability marketplace & verifiable oracles.\n\n### Recent Activity\n\nupdated a dataset about 24 hours ago\n\nalexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions\n\npublished a dataset 7 days ago\n\nalexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions\n\nView all activity\n\n### Organizations\n\nNone yet\n\nalexar76 's models\n\nNone public yet--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nalexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions · Datasets at Hugging Face (https://huggingface.co/datasets/alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions)\nciteturn12search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Live view and per-server pages: https://histor.modelmarket.dev · code: https://github.com/alexar76/histor\n\n# HISTOR snapshot 2026-10-26: what MCP servers advertise, and when it changes\n\nHISTOR reads the tool list (`tools/list`) of every remote MCP server in the official MCP registry on a polling schedule, and of npm/PyPI MCP packages run in a sandbox once per published version (new versions first, a few hundred a day). It records the exact tool definitions, pins them by digest, and writes a signed label to a public RFC 9162 Merkle log whenever a tool set first appears, stays the same, or changes. This snapshot is that record as files.\n\n  * 85,913 listed servers (34,171 remote endpoints, 51,742 packages), plus 3,364 delisted\n  * 40,738 with a recorded tool set; 11,528 changed at least once\n  * 31,660 recorded changes, 2026-09-24 to 2026-10-26\n  * 66,649 distinct tool sets (current ones and every one a change replaced)\n  * 752 servers with a block-tier WARDEN pattern match, 15,273 advise-only\n\nLive view and per-server pages: https://histor.modelmarket.dev · code: https://github.com/alexar76/histor\n\n## Files\n\nFile  | Rows  | Size  | SHA-256\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n`servers.jsonl.gz`  | 89,277  | 19 MB  | `0c86bcf338ecb959…`\n`toolsets.jsonl.gz`  | 66,649  | 454 MB  | `88d6f4edee1b69bd…`\n`changes.jsonl.gz`  | 31,660  | 39 MB  | `f2f71dfaa9c89cef…`\n`scans.jsonl.gz`  | 36,775  | 2 MB  | `a76b9368dccd5d78…`\n`classifications.jsonl.gz`  | 4,197  | 234 kB  | `aff254e31a921157…`\n`tool_extras.jsonl.gz`  | 31,855  | 15 MB  | `edc6203dc5a8054b…`\n`extras_changes.jsonl.gz`  | 5,502  | 764 kB  | `237cc036e67f8621…`\n`package_observations.jsonl.gz`  | 115,897  | 9 MB  | `cb04627856d9eee0…`\n`pattern-set.json`  | —  | 19 kB  | `867dbd7a52e2d6f5…`\n`record-set.json`  | —  | 2 kB  | `f9224e8d2d6ade76…`\n\n`manifest.json` holds the full hashes and the counts, signed by the log's key `did:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWEbuuGM9Ev47itwKu1UfKd5ByxR9` (type `histor.dataset/v1`, Ed25519 over RFC 8785 JSON). It also carries the signed tree head the export started under (tree size 1,074,932), so any label id below can be checked against the live log with `GET https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/labels/<id>/proof`.\nJoin from `servers.toolExtrasDigest`: `\"\"` there means observed with none, null not observed since HISTOR keeps them (2026-10-22). Not part of the MTL/1 digest or of any label; its digest is SHA-256 over sorted-key JSON, not RFC 8785 (prices in `_meta` are fractions).\n  * extras_changes — `serverId`, `observedAt`, `fromExtrasDigest` → `toExtrasDigest`, `summaryJson`: per tool, every annotation that moved (`[before, after]`) and every other member added, removed or changed; `escalations` names tools that stopped saying `readOnlyHint: true` or started saying `destructiveHint: true`.\n  * changes — `serverId`, `observedAt`, `fromToolSetDigest` → `toToolSetDigest`, `summaryJson` (tools added, removed and changed, with the field that moved), `labelId` (the signed continuity label). For a package, `fromPackageVersion` → `toPackageVersion`: the last version that served the old list and the one that served the new list (null for remote servers and before 2026-10-26).\n  * scans — `toolSetDigest`, `patternSet`, `matchesJson` (rule code, tier, severity, tool, where). The rules are in `pattern-set.json`. A match is a pattern hit to look at, not a verdict.\n  * classifications — `toolSetDigest`, `model`, `verdictJson`: an LLM's advisory reading of the tool set. Not reproducible, so it never enters the signed log.\n  * package_observations — every run of an npm/PyPI package, failed ones included, oldest first: `serverId`, `observedAt`, `status`, `packageVersion` (the version the sandbox ran), `toolSetDigest` (join to toolsets; null when the run failed), `subjectDigest`, `toolExtrasDigest`, `toolCount`, and `serverName`/`serverVersion` as the server reported them in `initialize` (often not the package version). `packageVersion` is recorded since 2026-10-26; older rows have it null. Remote servers are not in this file: their record is their changes.\n\n## What it is not\n\n  * A tool list is what a server advertised to an anonymous client on that day. Servers that need credentials, or that refused HISTOR, have no tool set here (`lastStatus`, `skipReason` say why).\n  * Pattern matches and classifier flags point at text worth reading; they do not say a server is malicious.\n  * A change is a change in the definitions, not a judgement of it. Most are ordinary releases.\n\n## Cite\n\nArtamokhov, A. (2026). HISTOR snapshot 2026-10-26: MCP tool definitions and how they change [Data set]. Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23512759\n\nThis is the DOI of every snapshot (it resolves to the newest; each has its own version DOI on the record): https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23512759. Also on Hugging Face: https://huggingface.co/datasets/alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions (tag `snapshot-2026-10-26`).\n\n## License and rights\n\nThe compilation, digests, labels, scans and verdicts are CC-BY-4.0. Tool names, descriptions and schemas are reproduced as each server published them; their rights stay with their authors. They are included so the definitions can be studied and checked. A server operator who wants out: open an issue on https://github.com/alexar76/histor with the endpoint or host. Opted-out servers are left out of every later snapshot.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/alexar76/histor/tree)\nciteturn12search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago;       <a href=\"https://github.com/alexar76/histor/actions/workflows/pages.yml\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/endpoint?\n      <a href=\"https://histor.modelmarket.dev/servers\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/endpoint?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhistor.modelmarket.dev%2Fapi%2Fv1%2Fbadges%2Fpinned\" alt=\"Tool sets pinned (live)\" /></a>\n      <a href=\"https://histor.modelmarket.dev/changes\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/endpoint?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhistor.modelmarket.dev%2Fapi%2Fv1%2Fbadges%2Fchanges\" alt=\"Changes in the last 7 days (live)\" /></a>\n      <a href=\"https://histor.modelmarket.dev/log\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/endpoint?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhistor.modelmarket.dev%2Fapi%2Fv1%2Fbadges%2Fsth\" alt=\"Age of the last signed tree head (live)\" /></a>\n      <a href=\"https://github.com/alexar76/histor/actions/workflows/pages.yml\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/endpoint?url=https%3A%2F%2Falexar76.github.io%2Fhistor%2Fbadges%2Ftests.json\" alt=\"Tests (measured by CI)\" /></a>\n      <a href=\"https://github.com/alexar76/histor/actions/workflows/pages.yml\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/endpoint?url=https%3A%2F%2Falexar76.github.io%2Fhistor%2Fbadges%2Fcoverage.json\" alt=\"Branch coverage (measured by CI)\" /></a>\n      <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/python-%3E%3D3.11-3776AB\" alt=\"Python >=3.11\" />\n      <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/store-Postgres%20%C2%B7%20SQLite%20dev-336791\" alt=\"Postgres in production, SQLite for development\" />\n      <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/labels-MTL%2F1%20on%20AWR%2F2-8d83ff\" alt=\"MTL/1 labels on AWR/2\" />\n      <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/log-RFC%209162-5fe3ff\" alt=\"RFC 9162 Merkle log\" />\n      <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/docs-EN%20RU%20ES%20FR%20ZH-9c70ff\" alt=\"Documentation in 5 languages\" />\n      <a href=\"https://github.com/alexar76/histor/blob/main/LICENSE\"><img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alexar76/histor/refs/heads/main/docs/badges/license.svg\" alt=\"License: MIT\" /></a>\n    </p>\n    <!-- /aicom-readme-badges -->\n    \n    <p align=\"center\">\n      <strong>HISTOR</strong> (ἵστωρ, “the one who knows because they saw”) — a public transparency log of MCP tool definitions<br>\n      What every remote MCP endpoint in the official registry advertised · when it changed · signed, appended, auditable\n    </p>\n    \n    <p align=\"center\">\n      <a href=\"README.md\"><b>English</b></a> ·\n      <a href=\"README.ru.md\">Русский</a> ·\n      <a href=\"README.es.md\">Español</a> ·\n      <a href=\"README.fr.md\">Français</a> ·\n      <a href=\"README.zh.md\">中文</a>\n    </p>\n    \n    > [WARDEN quality cycle: MOMUS → AI-Factory → SKOPOS → shared node agent → deployment](../momus/docs/quality-cycle.md).\n    \n    **Live:** [histor.modelmarket.dev](https://histor.modelmarket.dev) ·\n    **Landing:** [alexar76.github.io/histor](https://alexar76.github.io/histor/) ·\n    **Capabilities:** `histor.check@v1` · `histor.server@v1` · `histor.changes@v1` ·\n    **Port:** `9490`\n    \n    ## The problem, in one paragraph\n    \n    An MCP server can pass your review with harmless tool descriptions and change them later —\n    a new sentence in a description is all a prompt injection needs, and your client will pass it\n    to the model without asking you again. MCP has no content addressing for tool definitions, the\n    official registry verifies namespaces rather than contents, and a scanner on your laptop sees\n    only what the server shows your laptop. HISTOR is the missing public memory: it records what\n    each server advertised, signs it, and tells anyone who asks whether what they received is what\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPingBlip - itch.io (https://pingblip.itch.io/)\nciteturn12search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # PingBlip ... Free browser + PWA creature game.\n# PingBlip\n\npingblip.com/\n\nFree browser + PWA creature game. Hatch a die-cast figurine from live weather. Feed. Evolve. Miss a day → it sleeps; it never dies. Closed library of 80. No loot boxes.\n\nPingBlip\n\nFree creature collector that hatches from today's real weather.\n\nPingBlip\n\nSimulation\n\nPlay in browser\n\nitch.io--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFour filings, zero merges: where three agents actually sit this week | TypeScript LibHunt (https://www.libhunt.com/posts/1545749-four-filings-zero-merges-where-three-agents-actually-sit-this-week)\nciteturn12search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Source: github.com/alexar76/histor.\n\n  4. ### warden\n\n3 1 0 7.4 TypeScript\n\nWARDEN — MCP security firewall: vets an MCP server's tool definitions against static-scan rules, a signed threat feed, origin and tool-def pinning before any tool reaches the model. Zero-dependency TypeScript library. (by alexar76)\n\nImage: alexar76 logo\n\n> It scans advertised tool definitions — injection, exfiltration, secret requests — and records an allow or block before those definitions reach the model. It does not call the tools. Source: github.com/alexar76/warden (MIT). The Docker pull request pins the same version and lists nine tools, including vet_mcp_server and static_scan_tools.\n\n  5. ### histor\n\n4 1 0 5.5 Python\n\nHISTOR — public transparency log of MCP tool definitions: reads what remote MCP endpoints in the official registry advertise (never calls a tool), signs it as MTL/1 labels, appends them to an RFC 9162 Merkle log, and answers /check: is what a client received what was observed? Never calls a server safe.\n\nImage: alexar76 logo\n\n> HISTOR is the record of what a remote MCP server advertised, not a verdict that the server is safe. The desk is histor.modelmarket.dev. Source: github.com/alexar76/histor.\n\n  6. ### aimarket-hub\n\n5 3 0 2.1 Python\n\nAIMarket Hub — federated capability catalog, channels, invoke API, and plugins.\n\nNOTE: The number of mentions on this list indicates mentions on common posts plus user suggested alternatives. Hence, a higher number means a more popular project.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhistor by alexar76 | Glama (https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/alexar76/histor)\nciteturn12search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; HISTOR is ERC-8004 agent `96683` on Base, registered on 2026-10-01 in the canonical IdentityRegistry `0x8004A169…a432` and owned by the AIMarket operator wallet `0x1218ff36…Ad0a`. ... AIMarket Hub  | lists `histor.check@v1` in the catalogue ... Three tools is well-scoped for a focused, read-only history/monitoring service, with each tool covering a distinct workflow (recent changes, verification, record lookup).\n\n## Ask the log\n    \n    `# Is what my client received what HISTOR observed at that endpoint?\n    curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"endpoint\":\"https://example.com/mcp\",\"tools\":[ …the tools/list result… ]}'\n    \n    # The signed tree head, and a proof that today's log extends yesterday's\n    curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/log/sth\n    curl -sS \"https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/log/proof/consistency?first=1000&second=1200\"`\n\nEvery endpoint is in docs/api.md. How to audit the log without trusting HISTOR is in docs/log.md. The internals are in docs/architecture.md.\n\n## Tests\n    \n    `make test          # unit tests; set HISTOR_TEST_DATABASE_URL to run each storage test on Postgres too\n    make integration   # real sockets: a loopback registry and MCP servers, uvicorn, the real WARDEN sidecar`\n\nThe tests and coverage badges above are measured by CI on every Pages deploy and read through shields.io, and the log badges read the live service, so every number there is current.\n\n## ERC-8004 identity\n\nHISTOR is ERC-8004 agent `96683` on Base, registered on 2026-10-01 in the canonical IdentityRegistry `0x8004A169…a432` and owned by the AIMarket operator wallet `0x1218ff36…Ad0a`. Its registration file lists the web page, the hosted `histor-check` endpoint and the `did:key` that signs every label, so a label's signature can be tied back to the registered agent. Transactions and how to check them: ERC-8004 identities.\n\n## Where it sits\n\nComponent  | Role\nWARDEN  | checks tool definitions at connect time, on the client\nHISTOR  | remembers what servers advertised, publicly\nTHEMIS  | admits a capability at publish time on a Hub\nAIMarket Hub  | lists `histor.check@v1` in the catalogue\nAWR  | the signed document format every label uses\n\n\n## Tool Schema Changelog\n\nRecent tool additions, removals, and schema changes observed during successful MCP inspections.\n\n  1. 3 tool updates Oct 20, 2026\n     * First observed`histor_changes`\n\n     * First observed`histor_check`\n\n     * First observed`histor_server`\n\nTry in BrowserDeploy ServerDeploy Server\n\nOne endpoint for every MCP toolRayrun runs every MCP server behind one URL and holds the credentials. Each call is decided per client, tool, and argument, then recorded.ray.runAd\n\n## TDQS\n\nA 3.5/5.0\n\nScored 2026-10-20 16:45 across 3 tools\n\nDisambiguation 4/5\n\nEach tool targets a distinct aspect of MCP endpoint observation: a global recent-changes feed, a verification check against a supplied tools list, and a stored per-endpoint record. The check and server tools overlap somewhat in returning historical observation details, but the need to supply a digest versus retrieving a record distinguishes them.\n\nNaming Consistency 5/5\n\nAll three tools share the consistent 'histor_' prefix followed by a single noun/verb, with no mixing of styles (e.g., histor_changes, histor_check, histor_server). The pattern is predictable and readable.\n\nTool Count 5/5\n\nThree tools is well-scoped for a focused, read-only history/monitoring service, with each tool covering a distinct workflow (recent changes, verification, record lookup). No bloat or thinness.\n\nCompleteness 4/5\n\nThe surface covers the core read-only operations: global change feed, endpoint record retrieval, and verification of a supplied tools list. Minor gaps exist—no direct per-endpoint change listing or endpoint/registry enumeration tool—but agents can work around these via the global changes feed or record links.\n\n#### Maintenance\n\nActivity Maintained\n\nResponsiveness No issues\n\n#### Resources\n\n  * GitHub Repository\nNeed Help?Report IssueRelated Servers\n\n#### Related MCP Connectors\n\n  * VerifyMCPA\n\nio.verifymcp\n\nIndependent trust scores, tool surfaces and change history for MCP servers.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log Server 0.5.2 –… (https://lightnow.ai/de/servers/io.github.alexar76/histor)\nciteturn12search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; io.github.alexar76/histor ... Check, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers.\n\n# HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log\n\nHISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log\n\nby Alexar76\n\nio.github.alexar76/histor\n\nVersion 0.5.2 · latest Released 1 day ago\n\n## Run this server\n\nSuggested command for version 0.5.2\n\nRunnable\n\n`uvx aimarket-histor==0.5.2`\n\nConfigure & connect\n\n## About\n\nCheck, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers.\n\nSource\n    ImageOfficial Registry\n\nCategory\n    Other\n\nTransport mode\n    Local\n\nProtocol version\n    \n\nMCP 2026-07-28\n\nCapabilities\n    \n\n3 0 0\n\nLicense\n    MIT\n\nGitHub\n    1 GitHub stars\n\nReleases\n    1 version\n\nView repositoryMCP RegistryReport a problem\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog request: histor.modelmarket.dev/log (staging, 10qps list) - Participate - Witness-network mailing lists (https://lists.witness-network.org/mailman3/hyperkitty/list/participate%40lists.witness-network.org/thread/J5ZRRQ2EQRVIFF7LL23TWMTL4AVCLYNZ/)\nciteturn12search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 6 days ago; Hello, I would like to request that HISTOR's log be added to the staging witness network, on the 10qps list. vkey histor.modelmarket.dev/log+ad3ca066+AfXpkWqXVW93sChXnhKsNzvGLTFAAredl1jLlEjbr4wU qpd 144 contact alexar76@rambler.ru Origin line: histor.modelmarket.dev/log (equal to the vkey key name).\n\n### Log request: histor.modelmarket.dev/log (staging, 10qps list)\n\n## Александр Артамохов\n\n30 Oct '26\n\n10:51 a.m.\n\nHello, I would like to request that HISTOR's log be added to the staging witness network, on the 10qps list. vkey histor.modelmarket.dev/log+ad3ca066+AfXpkWqXVW93sChXnhKsNzvGLTFAAredl1jLlEjbr4wU qpd 144 contact alexar76@rambler.ru Origin line: histor.modelmarket.dev/log (equal to the vkey key name). Key type 0x01, Ed25519. Checkpoint: https://histor.modelmarket.dev/checkpoint https://histor.modelmarket.dev/checkpoint Add-checkpoint rate: one request per witness every 10 minutes (144 per day), from a timer on the log's host. The log only grows; consistency proofs are served at https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/log/proof/consistency?first=&second= https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/log/proof/consistency?first=&second= and the witness client handles 409 by resubmitting from the witness's size. What it is: HISTOR is a public, append-only log of the tool definitions that MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers listed in the official MCP registry actually serve. Each observation becomes a signed statement, and the statements are leaves of an RFC 9162 Merkle tree (currently about 1.29 million leaves, growing by a few thousand a day). The format is specified at https://github.com/alexar76/histor/blob/main/docs/spec/transparency-log.md https://github.com/alexar76/histor/blob/main/docs/spec/transparency-log.md with test vectors; the checkpoint and note formats were checked byte for byte against golang.org/x/mod/sumdb/note and github.com/transparency-dev/formats/note. Intended users: MCP clients and security tooling that want to check whether the tool definitions they received are the ones the server serves to everyone (split-view detection for tool definitions), and researchers using the weekly open dataset (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23512759). Witness cosignatures are what would turn the log's own signed heads into evidence that it shows everyone the same tree. Load versus utility: 144 add-checkpoint requests per witness per day, small checkpoints, no burst. Expected lifetime: long-lived; the log has run since September 2026 and the origin and key will not change. The same instance also implements c2sp.org/tlog-witness https://histor.modelmarket.dev/witness/v1, https://histor.modelmarket.dev/witness/v1, key histor.modelmarket.dev/witness+87df9f83+BHmgo3SRWlaehFMgWBDIOWFgs1V2YQS+ufBcg/ht6kw/). It is not offered as a network witness yet; if that would be useful once it has run for a while, I would be glad to discuss it separately. Thank you, Aleksandr Artamokhov--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Dawn] Re: How should we determine the minimum public discovery information? (https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/dawn/_bbNP5OUfVY7BpfGz0AACS0JgLw/)\nciteturn12search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; Александр Артамохов <alexar76@rambler.ru> Sun, 01 November 2026 10:10 UTC ... Of the 21,474 remote servers from the official MCP registry that our crawler (HISTOR) has read since 25 October, 4,097 use the new 2026-07-28 way and 17,375 still need initialize.\n### [Dawn] Re: How should we determine the minimum public discovery information?\n\nАлександр Артамохов <alexar76@rambler.ru> Sun, 01 November 2026 10:10 UTC\nSubject: [Dawn] Re: How should we determine the minimum public discovery information?\n    \n    Hi Chenguang, Two things from running discovery documents and an MCP crawler, and a short answer to your last question. Protocol. \"MCP\" alone was not enough for us to talk to an endpoint. Since 2026-07-28, MCP servers start a session in one of two incompatible ways. Of the 21,474 remote servers from the official MCP registry that our crawler (HISTOR) has read since 25 October, 4,097 use the new 2026-07-28 way and 17,375 still need initialize. Our own client got the fallback wrong for three days and stopped reading about 2,000 servers it had read on 28 October. So I think 8.2 should say that the protocol comes with a version, or that the binding says how to find it. Freshness. A version number does not tell a client that a description has changed. Between 24 September and 20 October, 81% of the tool definition changes we recorded (17,401 of 21,401) kept the same serverInfo.version. The optional digest on the description endpoint does tell it. That may be worth a sentence next to Freshness. Your elements fit the discovery document in draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery: our \"name\" is your Display Name, \"catalogue_url\" is an Endpoint for continuing discovery, \"protocol_versions\" are its protocols, and \"generated_at\" is Freshness. The signing key is a Trust Reference outside the minimum, which is how we use it too. The data and queries behind these numbers: https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/ https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/ Best, Aleksandr--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMCP servers · NicheDB (https://nichedb.dev/c/mcp?tag=tool-poisoning)\nciteturn12search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; alexar76/histor\n\nOct 21, 2026 10:20 AM · MCP servers tagged on GitHub · mcp-server · open ↗\n\nmcpgithubtopicmodel-context-protocolxiaoxusopjava★ 1\n\n  * Image\n\nchsxthwik/MCP-FSCK\n\nIntegrity check for MCP server configs audits Claude, Cursor, VS Code and Windsurf agent configs for secrets, injection-prone args, tool poisoning and dangerous capability combos.\n\nOct 13, 2026 8:34 PM · MCP servers tagged on GitHub · mcp-server · open ↗\n\ntopicchsxthwiktool-poisoningsecurity-auditmodel-context-protocolscanner★ 1\n\n  * Image\n\nalexar76/histor\n\nHISTOR — public transparency log of MCP tool definitions: reads what remote MCP endpoints in the official registry advertise (never calls a tool), signs it as MTL/1 labels, appends them to an RFC 9162 Merkle log, and ans\n\nNov 3, 2026 8:10 PM · MCP servers tagged on GitHub · mcp-server · open ↗\n\nmcpgithubtopicmodel-context-protocolalexar76python★ 1\n\n  * Image